<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1555">1555</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Supreme Council of Italy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was a council of the Spanish monarchy, created under Philip II in the 1550s to advise the king on the government of his Italian possessions: the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Sitting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, it dealt with appointments, finance, justice and petitions from these states. It lost its purpose when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gave up its Italian lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>.</p></div>
